  <p>Our research examines how plankton communities are organized, transported, and transformed in a changing ocean. We combine field observations, autonomous platforms, satellite and modeled data, imaging systems, and computational analysis to connect biological patterns with the environmental conditions that shape them.</p>

  <h2 id="biogeography">Plankton Biogeography</h2>

  <p>Marine biogeography is often described using regional frameworks that divide the ocean into provinces, ecoregions, or assessment regions. These maps provide useful context, but plankton habitats are also shaped by moving gradients in salinity, nutrients, light, temperature, and water-column structure. We study these habitats as dynamic biological-environmental states rather than fixed boundaries on a map.</p>

